00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.690
Leah: So, what is a database?

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:08.930
A database is an ‘organised structured
collection of data’ which can be easily managed.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:16.050
The data is normally organised in tables which
contain rows, columns, and indexes that make it

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.900
easier to search and find relevant information.

00:00:19.459 --> 00:00:24.349
There are many different types of databases
but before we go into these first let’s first

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.340
start by looking at ‘unstructured data’.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.919
Chris: Unstructured data is not organised
and can be stored in a number of ways.

00:00:33.589 --> 00:00:38.720
For example, a letter has ‘structure’ in
the way it is arranged on the page [e.g.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:46.379
Date, address, salutation, heading, letter
content, signature etc], but the actual ‘data’

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.949
in the letter would be ‘unstructured’ so would
not be able to be manipulated in any way.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:59.280
Leah: However, if that same letter was used as a template
for a mail-merge, then the data that would be used to

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:06.479
generate the output letters, would in fact be ‘structured
data’ as the data would be stored in a structured way

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.079
as in a table such names and addresses of recipients.

00:01:10.879 --> 00:01:14.870
Here are some items that would be
classified as unstructured data:

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.240
• Chris: Emails – these could be business or personal

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.230
• Document files such as letters, reports, presentations

00:01:24.059 --> 00:01:31.720
• Websites – most web content is regarded as unprocessed
and unstructured, and on some websites the unstructured

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:36.370
data could be used for SEO [Search Engine Optimisation].

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.370
• Media such as images, video, or audio

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.520
• Mobile data such as text messages

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.619
• Communication such as team chats

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:53.820
• Social media – although technically a website this
is a little different as most social media websites

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.150
are considered both unstructured and structured data.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.080
There are some elements which is unstructured data
such as a user post, but there are other elements which

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.480
would be stored as structured data such as user details.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:13.980
Leah: It is important to recognise the distinction
between ‘structured’ and ‘structured data’ as many of the

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:20.169
these ‘items’ we have mentioned are in fact ‘structured’
like a letter, but their data is ‘unstructured’.

00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:24.730
Nearly all types of Databases
contain data that is ‘structured’.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:32.730
By structured we mean data that can be organised,
analysed, indexed, searched, and easy to understand.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.480
Chris: Here is a table of data that is ‘unstructured’

00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:42.609
Now if we were to ‘structure’ this data it would
look like this and be much easier to understand.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:50.220
There are many different types of databases used today
and most are enclosed within a database management

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:59.220
system [DBMS] within which the data is stored in tables
and can be manipulated or analysed using queries.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.070
Leah: A database uses structured
query language [SQL] to do this.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:14.950
SQL is often used by businesses to run statistical reports,
so, for example a college could generate a report from their

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:21.960
‘learning database’ using a query to ask, ‘how many learners
are studying a specific course in a specific month’.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:30.329
Databases make it easy to store information that is quick
and easy to search for, due to their index capabilities,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.019
and most websites use databases to store data.

00:03:34.859 --> 00:03:41.430
A lot of websites use MySQL which is an
‘open-source’ Relational database system.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:49.519
Chris: Nonrelational databases [NoSQL] have become
more popular in recent years as they offer a more

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:56.709
flexible, scalable alternative to the traditional
SQL ‘Relational database management system’ [RDBMS].

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.509
RDBMS work better for data that is more simple and static.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:12.360
NoSQL databases allow the use of ‘unstructured data’
whereas RDBMS are table based and therefore structured.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:18.130
So, for example, in the case of websites and
social media, the unstructured data could

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.430
be used for SEO or marketing campaigns.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.069
Leah: Cloud databases are stored on the
‘Cloud’ which could be private, public or a

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:34.380
hybrid cloud platform and there are two types
– ‘traditional’ or ‘Database as a service’ [DBaaS].

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:43.470
Open source databases can be SQL or NoSQL
and as we have said one of the most well

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.749
used is ‘MySQL’ which is an ‘RDBMS’.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:54.620
Other common database types include
‘Object-oriented’, ‘Distributed’, ‘Graph’,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.640
‘OLTP databases’ and ‘Data warehouse’.

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:04.739
Less common types which are more specific to their
areas of work and specialism include ‘Multi-model’,

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:09.700
‘Document/JSON’, and ‘Self-driving databases’.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.650
Chris: Check out our website section
on this page to find out more.

